#
# One file, one concern. It lives beside the check rather than inside it because the check crossed 300 lines
# when the seventh mutation was added (CLAUDE.md's cap is written for Swift and Python and `check-line-cap`
# scans *.swift only, but a 330-line gate nobody can hold in their head is the thing the cap exists to stop).
#
# Each row mutates ONE structural fact in a throwaway copy of apps/ios, re-runs the check against that copy,
# and requires it to refuse AND to name the reason. A check nobody has seen refuse is a check nobody has
# tested (CLAUDE.md). A row that exits 0, or exits non-zero without naming its expected reason, fails the
# table and the table's exit status is 1.
#
# The copy is the whole of apps/ios, not just the feature sources, since PR #101's finding B2: the call
# graph is decided over every *.swift under the app tree, so a row has to be able to mutate the app shell
# (row 9) as well as the feature. The check is handed both halves explicitly - `--sources <copy>/<FS>` for
# the named files, `--app-tree <copy>` for the call graph.
#
# Reached through `bash ops/lib/check-safety-disclaimer --prove-red`, which first refuses to run a table
# against a tree that is already red. Running this file directly does the same work without that guard.
#
# Row 11 is the only one whose sed script carries TWO commands: it MOVES the single write to the
# acknowledgement out of `SafetyDisclaimer(onAccept: {` and up to an `.onAppear` on the same screen, which
# keeps the occurrence count at one and is refused by the brace-depth half of the B4 anchor alone. `|` is
# the field separator here, so a script must be spelled with `/` delimiters and no pipe in it.
#
#   ROW = label | file under the apps/ios copy | sed script | substring the refusal must contain
set -uo pipefail
cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"

